           Guardian v1.2 (C)1998 by Stefano Coletta (alias Creator)
                        & Lorenzo Colitti (alias Lor)

                    **** Works only with CNet v4.32+ ****

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
                                WHAT'S THIS?!
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Are  you  tired of coming back home only to see CNet crashed and your BBS down
for  the count?  Well, check out Guardian and this problem will become a thing
of the past!

This  is  not  a  Rexx door nor is it a CNetC program, it is a guardian...  it
hunts  for  crashed  nodes, tries to revive them and, if necessary, resets the
system  without  human  intervention.   How?  By monitoring the status of each
loaded  port and taking the correct moves to reset the Amiga SAFELY.  Guardian
is  able  to  catch  the  majority  of  node lockups because it is an EXTERNAL
program  detached  from  CNet.   It  will  NEVER  interfere  with  normal CNet
operation since a port is crashed and it uses less than 0.01% CPU time.

This is what Guardian does:

1)  Monitors all loaded ports (whether a user is online or not) to see if they
    are locked  up.
2)  If a  node  crashes, Guardian tries to unlock or close and then reload it.
    If it succeeds, it goes back to monitoring.
3)  If not, it  immediately closes all loaded nodes that are IDLE (where there
    is no user online) and shuts down CNet related tasks (ie. Yank-Task, etc.)
4)  It  then sends two  OLMs telling  remaining  online users to logout within
    a configurable period of time.
5)  Sends a mail message to every kicked user to inform them why they have been
    disconnected. The crashed user also receives a message asking for a
    detailed report of what he/she was doing before the crash.
6)  It waits for all users to log out before closing the remaining ports; if a
    user  does  not log out within a  specified  (configurable) period of time,
    Guardian closes the port anyway. The user was warned... 
    If a user logs out of his own accord, CNet closes the port automatically.
7)  When  all ports  except the locked  one are idle Guardian unmounts ALL devs
    through an external utility and resets the Amiga.
8)  In rare instances Guardian is able to close  all the ports and reload them
    without forcing a reset.

Everything Guardian does is logged.  The default file is SYSDATA:Log/Guardian,
but this can be changed by editing Guardian.cfg.

  This program is the final solution you have always wanted for your CNet BBS.
Well, you won't know if you don't try it, will you?! :-)


Limits
------

*  Guardian will not catch frozen CNetC or Arexx doors that are not responding
   due to endless loops or similar problems.

*  Guardian does not support TrapDoor. Coming soon...

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
                                 INSTALLATION
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Quite simple, actually...

A beginner's guide:
 1. Install ShutAll following the instructions included.
 2. Copy the Guardian directory into DOORS: .
 3. Edit the Guardian.cfg and Delay.cfg files to suit your needs.
 4. Edit CNet-Startup so it starts Guardian after Control at every boot.
 5. All done, it should work... :-)


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
                    UPGRADING FROM *ANY* PREVIOUS VERSION
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

                 YOU NEED TO REINSTALL THE ENTIRE PACKAGE! :(



------------------------------------------------------------------------------
                                 THE PROGRAM
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

There are THREE executables:

Guardian (the  main  program). This one must be started AFTER Cnet is loaded.
Launch it with the normal RUN command from CLI, or put it in your CNet-Startup
after control.

e.g.:

   run <>NIL:  Control
   Cd DOORS:Guardian
   Run <>NIL: Guardian WAIT QUIET

The  "cd doors:guardian" statement is necessary because Guardian looks for its
configuration file in the current directory.  Ok, I'm lazy, so shoot me.  :-)

GCmd  (short  for  Guardian  Commander)  is used to send commands to Guardian.
Guardian will be completely invisible after it starts up, and with this little
utility you can quit Guardian or query it to see if it is alive.  You can also
obtain information such as uptime from last boot or crash and a rather useless
"Guardian Performance Index", which measures the load on your system.  A score
of 100 represents optimal performance.

TaskCloser is the executable which Guardian uses to close CNet-related tasks.
It can also be run as a stand-alone program.

GCmd has four command line options:

INFO     - Uptime info and Guardian Performance Index
QUIT     - To quit the Guardian
QUERY    - To check if Guardian is alive
SHUTDOWN - Performs a safe CNet & system shutdown. Note that this is a safer
           way to reboot the system than simply rebooting. If you have set an
           event which periodically reboots the system, you might want to
           change the event so it runs GCmd SHUTDOWN instead.

Guardian has two command line options:

WAIT     - Wait 2 minutes for CNet to load.
QUIET    - Suppresses usual messages from displaying.

TaskCloser accepts one command line option: the NAME of the port associated
with the task. For example, if you want to close Yank-Task, you could do:

     TaskCloser cnyank_serv

Note that the name of the port is case sensitive!


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
                           THE CONFIGURATION FILES
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Here is an example Guardian.cfg file (the default one, in fact):

--------------------8<---------------8<--------------8<-----------------------
SYSDATA:Log/Guardian
Run SYS:Rexxc/Rx DOORS:Guardian/ClosePort.rexx %d
Run <>NIL: CNET:bbs %d
Run SYS:ShutAll/ShutAll
*** WARNING! A critical error has occurred. Port %d has crashed. Shutdown sequence has commenced. You have THREE minutes to logoff.
*** WARNING! You will be disconnected in ONE minute. This is your last chance to logoff normally!
*** System shutdown sequence commenced! You have THREE minutes to logoff.      *** System shutdown imminent! You will be disconnected in ONE minute. This is your last chance to logoff normally!
Run <>NIL: DOORS:Guardian/DosOLM T=%d M=%s
Run <>NIL: DOORS:Guardian/TaskCloser %s
Run <>NIL: CNET:mail-task
Run <>NIL: CNET:file-task
Run <>NIL: CNET:yank-task
Run <>NIL: CNET:news-task
Run <>NIL: CNET:cnsmtpd
--------------------8<---------------8<--------------8<-----------------------

           ***  NOTE THAT THE LINES MUST NOT BE LONGER
                THAN 255 CHARACTERS, INCLUDING COMMENTS!  ***

Let's start from the beginning, line by line:
1. Complete path of the Guardian log file. Check it out, it's very informative
   and can be fun to read!
2. Command line which Guardian will use to close ports with the AREXX script.
3. Command line which Guardian will use to load ports.
4. Command line of the external program which Guardian  will use to unmount all
   devices prior to rebooting. We highly recommend you install the included
   ShutAll archive.
5. First warning message sent to online users when a port crashes.
6. Second  (last  chance)  warning  message  sent  to  online users when a port
   crashes.
7. First warning message sent to online users when GCmd tells Guardian to 
   perform a shutdown.
8. Second  (last  chance)  warning  message  sent  to  online users when GCmd
   tells Guardian to perform a shutdown.
9. Command line of the DosOLM program, which Guardian will use to send OLMs.
10.Command line of the TaskCloser which Guardian will use to close CNet related
   tasks.
11.Command line to run the the Mail-Task.
12.Command line to run the the File-Task.
13.Command line to run the the Yank-Task.
14.Command line to run the the News-Task.
15.Command line to run the the Cnet SMTP daemon.


Here is an example Delay.cfg file (the default one, in fact):


--------------------8<---------------8<--------------8<-----------------------
120
60
20
40
60
15
5
20
30
--------------------8<---------------8<--------------8<-----------------------

           ***  NOTE THAT THE LINES MUST NOT BE LONGER
                THAN 255 CHARACTERS, INCLUDING COMMENTS!  ***

Let's start from the beginning, line by line:
1. Time in seconds between first and last warnings to online users.
2. Time  in seconds  between  the  last warning and forced logout (if you don't
   logout, you'll get kicked out!).
3. Time, in  seconds, that  Guardian  will  wait  for  the  external program to
   unmount all devices before rebooting.
4. If  Guardian succeeds in closing all ports and CNet-related tasks after a
   crash, it will attempt to reload them again,  assuming that the crash  was
   due to port conflicts. This is the time in  seconds which  Guardian will
   wait for CNet to reload all the ports and tasks before giving up and
   rebooting. Note that Guardian will only reload those ports and tasks which
   were running when the crash occurred.
5. This is the time in seconds which Guardian will wait for all ports and
   CNet-related tasks to close. See line 4.
6. If a port crashes, one of the first things that Guardian tries to do is
   close it. This is the time Guardian will wait before giving up on the port
   and resort to more effective measures.
7. This is the time Guardian will wait for the port to reload in the unlikely
   event that the attempt to close the crashed port succeeds. See line 6.
8. If the WAIT command line option is specified, this is the number of times
   which Guardian will check to see if Control is running on startup. An
   attempt is made every six seconds; if all these attempts fail Guardian will
   exit and report "CNet Control is not running".
9. If a port seems to crash when there is no one online, Guardian will wait
   a bit before checking the port again to make sure it has really crashed
   (unlikely if no one is online!). This is the number of seconds it will
   wait.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
                                   HISTORY
------------------------------------------------------------------------------


01/02/1998: v1.2 - Major changes/bugfixes release

            A massive bug was fixed! Due to silly variable declarations,
            buffers were being copied into arrays which were too small for
            them, guaranteeing a software failure... oh well.

            Run statement was missing in the first line of Guardian.cfg.  :)

            GCmd no longer hangs if Guardian is busy fighting crashes.
            It now reports "Guardian is busy".

            Guardian now accepts comments in config files.

            Config file split in two files:
            - Main configuration file: "Guardian.cfg"
            - Delay configuration file: "Delay.cfg". This contains ALL delay
              values to adapt Guardian to your configuration and system speed.

            Hard coding for optimization: code reduced, functions smarter and
            faster than ever!

            Crash resolution routine completely rewritten: now it is ready for
            TrapDoor and/or other add-ons.

            Improved Guardian's strategy. Guardian now identifies and
            attempts to solve many problems which it wouldn't see before.

            Ports that  have been closed  by CNet (due to a normal user logoff)
            are now logged - finally!

            Now Guardian takes care to shut down all CNet related tasks before
            rebooting. Check out the TaskCloser command; it may also be used
            as a stand-alone program, separately from Guardian.

            Fixed multiple port crash crisis situations: Guardian no longer
            hangs after trying to send an OLM to a crashed port.

            Fixed a bug in the log routine: events after date changes are now
            logged correctly.

            Removed Guardian's dependency from Runport.rexx script.
            You may delete the runport.rexx script.

            Enforcer Hit removed from Guardian.

            Guardian now sends a mail message to every kicked user to inform
            them why they have been disconnected. The crashed user also
            receives a message asking for a detailed report of what he/she was
            doing before the crash. The text of these messages is in the files
            kick.msg and crash.msg.

            Enforcer Hit removed from GCmd.

            WAIT command line option added. If WAIT is specified, Guardian
            will wait a user-defined time for CNet Control to load. The WAIT
            statement in CNet-Startup is no longer necessary.

            Improved Guardian logging.

            Guardian now checks to see if Control has been closed while it is
            running. If Guardian sees that Control is no longer active it will
            exit.

            Fixed trash display in "Kicking out user..." log entry.

            Added SHUTDOWN command to GCmd. With this function you can safely
            reboot your machine with the same method Guardian uses.

12/01/1998: v1.1a - Bug fix release (not released)

            In v1.1 GCmd was the same as the one in v1.0 due to an archive
            packing error.

            Bug fixed in reporting uptime status. Now minutes are properly
            displayed.

11/01/1998: v1.1 - Upgrading release

            Better handling for crashed ports with no one on.

            Added hours display to the "uptime" report generated by Guardian.

            Now GCmd is responsible for handling Guardian output, so Guardian
            is completely invisible.

            Added an icon for Guardian.

            Improved log with more details:

            - Added WHERE and USERNAME info when kicking out users
            - NEW: Guardian now logs when it is started.
            - NEW: Guardian now logs when it is closed by GCmd.
            - NEW: Before rebooting, Guardian logs uptime

06/01/1998: v1.0 - First release

            After 15 hours of hard work, v1.0 sees the light of day. There's
            no going back now...


